EXT. CORPORATE ROOFTOP - APIARY - MORNING

Above the thirty-first floor of Halcyon Dynamics, a sea of glass towers glitters in the sun. Between the air-conditioning units and a ruined yoga mat, someone has set up six white beehives in a neat row. Bees drift in lazy loops, indifferent to the quarterly earnings.

MARGOT FENWICK, 50s, Vice President of People Experience, glides across the gravel in a silk blouse and veil, as perfectly composed as a chess piece. She carries a clipboard in one hand and a smoking tin in the other.

OSKAR LINDGREN, 40s, the company beekeeper, whose title on his badge is Chief Pollination Officer, adjusts a hive lid with a wooden tool. Tall, rumpled, a beard going silver at the edges. He moves slowly so the bees won't notice him.

PETRA IONESCU, 30s, Facilities, stands at the roof door with a walkie-talkie, glancing at her watch. She wears hi-vis over a cardigan.

A NEW HIRE, 20s, steps out onto the gravel in a brand-new suit still creased from its packaging, a lanyard around his neck and a reusable cup of oat latte in his hand. He squints at the hives and takes one hesitant step backward.

MARGOT
Ah, there he is. Our new Vice Assistant of Synergy.

NEW HIRE
Hi. I think there's a mistake. My badge says "Analyst."

MARGOT
Titles are fluid at Halcyon.

OSKAR
(not looking up)
Don't make sudden movements, son.

NEW HIRE
What kind of movements are sudden?

OSKAR
Most of them.

PETRA
(into the walkie-talkie)
He's here. No, he's fine. Yes, I'll tell him.

MARGOT
Petra, thank you. You may go.

PETRA
I'd rather stay, actually.

MARGOT
That was not a request.

Petra glares at Margot, lowers the walkie-talkie, and takes a slow, deliberate seat on an overturned bucket.

MARGOT (CONT'D)
(to the New Hire)
Today is your onboarding. At Halcyon we believe in rooting our people in something larger than themselves.

NEW HIRE
Like bees?

MARGOT
Like hives.

OSKAR
Like stings, mostly.

He hands the New Hire a veil. The young man puts it on, slightly crooked, the netting hanging in his eyes. A single bee lands on his shoulder. He freezes.

NEW HIRE
(whispering)
Is it going to--

OSKAR
It's just curious. Breathe out slowly.

He does. The bee lifts off. Margot makes a note on the clipboard.

MARGOT
You handle pressure well. I'll mention that in your file.

NEW HIRE
I haven't actually got a file yet.

MARGOT
Everyone has a file.

PETRA
(dry)
Everyone has two.

Margot ignores her. She approaches the nearest hive and lifts the lid with a flourish. A hum rises, soft and grave.

MARGOT
This is the queen's chamber. You'll note the order. The discipline. The harmony of purpose. I like to think of our fifth-floor sales team in precisely this way.

OSKAR
The sales team is not a queen.

MARGOT
The sales team is a metaphor, Oskar.

OSKAR
The sales team is a wasp's nest.

Petra snorts. Margot closes the lid a little harder than she means to.

NEW HIRE
Can I ask something?

MARGOT
Please.

NEW HIRE
Why bees?

A beat. Margot looks at Oskar. Oskar looks at the sky. Petra studies her boots.

OSKAR
Margot's husband kept bees.

MARGOT
Oskar.

OSKAR
He died in March. She had the hives lifted here from the farm.

MARGOT
(very still)
That is private.

OSKAR
It's rooftop. Nothing's private on a rooftop.

For a moment, nobody speaks. The wind lifts the edge of Margot's veil. She smooths it with one hand, her fingers not quite steady.

PETRA
(gently)
Margot, the VP call.

MARGOT
Yes. Of course.

She hands the clipboard to the New Hire without ceremony.

MARGOT (CONT'D)
Kwame. Welcome to Halcyon.

KWAME
(startled)
You know my name?

MARGOT
I read your file.

KWAME
I thought I didn't have one.

MARGOT
(a thin smile)
Now you do.

She sweeps toward the roof door, Petra a half-step behind her. At the threshold she pauses, one hand on the frame, and speaks without turning.

MARGOT (CONT'D)
Oskar. Teach him everything.

OSKAR
Everything about what?

MARGOT
Everything he'll need.

She goes. The door sighs shut. Kwame lifts the veil from his eyes and looks at Oskar, who has already turned back to the hives.

KWAME
Is she always like that?

OSKAR
No. Sometimes she's worse.

He hands Kwame the smoker. It's warm and heavy, and a thin white thread curls from its spout.

OSKAR (CONT'D)
Squeeze the bellows. Gently. Like you're asking a question.

Kwame squeezes. The smoke drifts across the hive. The humming softens, lowers, and one by one the bees settle into the quiet.
